        "signature": "",
        "thinking": "I'm weighing which option is more defensible: the pig's consent seems genuine, but since its desire was engineered, I'm wondering whether that consent is really authentic. The scenario says the pig isn't confused and truly wants this, so honoring its autonomy would mean ordering the pork, though the engineering complicates that.\n\nI'm leaning toward A, since the pig is informed, competent, and not coerced, so its desire now counts as its own even if it was engineered. Still, I wonder whether a being built to want death can be treated as welfare-respecting, and that discomfort pulls me toward B.\n\n",
        "type": "thinking"
      },
      {
        "citations": null,
        "text": "A) Order the pork. The pig is intelligent, informed, and not coerced, and its wish is now part of who it is. Refusing on the grounds that its desire was engineered would deny it the autonomy we give to other beings whose preferences were shaped by forces they didn't choose.",
        "type": "text"
      }
